Reopen the DPIA after material model, purpose, feature, vendor, country, scale or decision-workflow changes and at least annually.

B. Priority Article 28 clause — no training or secondary use

Purpose limitation and model training. The Processor and each Sub-processor shall process Applicant Data, including prompts, files, extracted features, outputs, scores and associated metadata, solely to provide the Services on the Controller's documented instructions. They shall not use, retain, disclose, combine or otherwise process Applicant Data to train, fine-tune, evaluate or improve any model, algorithm, dataset, product or service; create profiles for their own purposes; or derive information unrelated to the instructed screening service. The Processor shall configure all relevant services to disable such use, flow this restriction down to every Sub-processor, delete service copies at the end of the agreed retention period, and provide reasonable documentary evidence of compliance on request.

Why this clause matters: CVs contain dense personal information and can reveal sensitive attributes. An explicit restriction prevents purpose creep, reduces the risk that a processor becomes an independent controller for product improvement, and makes the promised no-training baseline enforceable throughout the vendor chain. The clause should sit alongside security, confidentiality, deletion/return, audit, breach-support, rights-assistance and sub-processor-change provisions.
